course: dictionary
lesson: 11097
title: Wheelchairs and respect
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A wheelchair is a chair with large wheels. People who cannot walk, or find walking hard, use one to get around. Some wheelchairs are pushed by the user's arms, some are pushed by another person, and some have electric motors.
explain: Wheelchair is a noun, so you can say a wheelchair or wheelchairs. A person who uses one is a wheelchair user. You may hear phrases like «in a wheelchair», «wheelchair access» and «wheelchair-accessible», which means a place people in wheelchairs can get into and use.
explain: Respect means treating people as equals. «A boy in Toronto whose classmate uses a wheelchair treats her like anyone else, and asks "Would you like help?" before pushing her chair.» Asking first is good manners, because it lets the person decide what they need.
fact: A wheelchair is a chair with large wheels for people who cannot walk or find walking hard.
fact: Some wheelchairs are pushed by the user's arms, some by another person, and some have electric motors.
fact: Wheelchair access means ramps, lifts, wide doors and dropped kerbs that help wheelchair users get into and around places.
fact: Do not lean on or push someone's wheelchair without asking, because it is part of their personal space.
fact: Speak to the person in the wheelchair, not to their helper.
exercise: «A girl in Vancouver meets her dad's friend who uses a wheelchair.» How does she make the chat fair? | She ignores him and talks to his carer | She shouts across the room | She pushes his chair closer | *She sits down so they are at the same eye level
exercise: Which is good manners when you meet someone in a wheelchair? | Pushing the chair straight away to be helpful | *Asking "Would you like help?" before you touch or push the chair | Speaking only to the person beside them | Leaning on the chair while you talk
exercise: Which of these helps a wheelchair user get into a building? | A step with no other way in | *A wheelchair ramp | A bike parked across the path | A bin left in the doorway
test: Which is not a way a wheelchair can be moved? | *Carried by a flying drone | Pushed by the user's arms | Moved by an electric motor | Pushed by another person
test: Why should you not park bikes or bins in the way of a ramp? | Because ramps are only for bikes | *Because a wheelchair user needs the ramp to get through | Because bins make ramps slippery | Because it spoils the look of the street
exam: Which phrase describes a place that a wheelchair user can get into and use easily? | Wheelchair motor | *Wheelchair-accessible | Wheelchair basketball | Wheelchair ramp steps
